WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.540
Consider line 'p' and line 'q' which are on a plane.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.280
The lines are also parallel to each other,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.460
which would mean that they do not intersect or ever meet.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.800
And this is transversal 'R' ,

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.620
which cuts the lines 'p' and 'q' at two distinct points.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:24.400
In this video we will try to understand the different types of angles formed

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.660
by the transversal with two parallel lines.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.540
The transversal forms four angles with line 'p',

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:37.480
angles 1, 2, 3 &amp; 4. And four angles with line 'q',

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:41.120
angles 5, 6, 7 &amp; 8.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.760
Let's move on to the first type of angles.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:50.220
If lines p and q are parallel, then angle 1 will always equal angle 5.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.320
This angle here will be equal to this one.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.180
They are called corresponding angles.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.280
It doesn't matter how the transversal is placed.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.420
These angles will always be equal.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.600
On a new page let us try moving the transversal around.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.460
Assume line 'a', and line 'b' are parallel to each other.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.940
And here is a transversal 'c'.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.520
You can see that these two angles look equal.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.880
Now let's rotate the transversal towards the right.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.220
These two angles again seem to be equal.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.340
Now let's rotate the transversal towards the left .

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.300
The angles become obtuse and are still equal.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.700
This tells us that the corresponding angles are always equal.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.600
Now let's go back to the previous page.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.220
Now let me ask you an interesting question.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.380
Look at the figure closely.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.180
Is this the only pair of corresponding angles?

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.460
No! It isn't. We have three more pairs of corresponding angles.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.800
When a transversal intersects a pair of Parallel lines.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.180
Four pairs of corresponding angles are formed.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.980
Two and six form a corresponding angles pair.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.660
So angle two will always equal angle six.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:09.340
You can even look at the figure and observe that they are equal.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.840
Similarly, angle three will equal angle seven,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.300
and angle four will equal angle eight.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.040
These are the four pairs of corresponding angles that are formed.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:26.040
1 &amp; 5, 2 &amp; 6, 3 &amp; 7, 4 &amp; 8.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.060
Angles 3, 4, 5 &amp; 6 are interior angles,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.760
because they are between the two parallel lines.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.920
And they are classified into two types.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.500
Let's understand the first type.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.780
Angle 3 and Angle 5 form a pair of alternate interior angles.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.080
We know why they are called interior,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.860
but why exactly are they called alternate?

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.920
If we have a round table with numbers 3, 4, 5 &amp; 6 around it,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.600
then 5 is alternate to 3.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.900
That's because after 3 you skip 1 position and take the next.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.900
Similarly, 6 will be alternate to 4.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.740
In this diagram as well

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.780
to find alternate of 3 we skip 1 position

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.040
and take the next which is 5 .

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:19.800
And similarly, angle 4 and angle 6 are also called alternate interior angles.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.740
This was the first type of interior angles.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.820
The second type is a pair in which

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.980
the angles are next to each other on the same side of the transversal.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:35.320
Like angle 3 and angle 6. They are called consecutive interior angles.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.440
Look at the figure now.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.520
Do you see any other pair of consecutive interior angles?

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.460
Yes! With the same logic even angles 4 &amp; 5

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:47.800
are consecutive interior Angles.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.220
Remember, there are two types of interior angles.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.540
Alternate and Consecutive. For alternate

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.600
we skip one and move to the next.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.060
For consecutive, we take the one that's immediately next to it.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.580
And the last types of angles that are formed are vertically opposite angles.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.760
Angles 1 &amp; 3 are called vertically opposite angles.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.780
In all there are 4 pairs of vertically opposite angles,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.180
Apart from angles 1 &amp; 3, we have angles 2 &amp; 4,

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.500
angles 5 &amp; 7 and angles 6 &amp; 8 as the other 3 pairs.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.740
And vertically opposite angles are always equal.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.040
These were the different types of angles that were formed

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.240
when a transversal meets two parallel lines.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.460
On a new page let's look at the beauty of these angles.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.200
Assume we have lines 'a' and 'b' which are parallel.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:48.860
And transversal 'c' which intersects them at two distinct points.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.260
As we already know the transversal forms eight angles.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.260
One, two, three, four, five, six,

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.400
seven and eight.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.640
Let us quickly analyze the relation between various angles formed.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.560
It's pretty simple actually.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.780
Angle two will be equal to angle six

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.960
as their corresponding angles.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.960
An angle eight equals angle six

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:16.360
as it is vertically opposite to it .

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.400
And angle four equals angle eight because they are corresponding angles.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:26.960
Similarly, angle one equals angle five as their corresponding angles.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.100
Angle seven equals angle five because they are vertically opposite.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:37.200
And angle three equals angle seven because they are corresponding angles.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.580
So if lines 'a' and 'b' are parallel,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.360
we can see that angles one, three, five and seven are equal.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:49.720
And angles two, four, six and eight are equal.

